1
00:00:38,110 --> 00:00:40,540
You're in need of correction,
aren't you?

2
00:00:41,630 --> 00:00:42,630
Yeah.

3
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:53,670
I might leave marks.

4
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:56,460
Not a great idea.

5
00:00:56,490 --> 00:00:57,970
That's not a no.

6
00:01:11,310 --> 00:01:12,640
That's got to burn.

7
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:15,000
Let me fix it.

8
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:46,425
See, what you have to understand,
Michael,

9
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:48,540
is that this is a fucked up free zone.

10
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:51,425
Guys who sit in Chuck's
chair become mayor.

11
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:52,550
Governor.

12
00:01:52,770 --> 00:01:54,790
We have to be beyond reproach.

13
00:01:55,060 --> 00:01:58,600
So no tinder at the goddamn office,
okay?

14
00:02:02,690 --> 00:02:05,265
It feels like yesterday I
was telling you all this.

15
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:06,660
Mm, 18 months ago.

16
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:08,175
Added some stuff of my own in there.

17
00:02:08,190 --> 00:02:09,970
Yeah. Tinder. I heard.

18
00:02:10,410 --> 00:02:11,730
Come on. He's waiting.

19
00:02:15,140 --> 00:02:17,195
Well, off the record, the,
uh, Attorney General's

20
00:02:17,210 --> 00:02:21,130
always happy to take my recommendations
on who to appoint to the federal bench.

21
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:24,780
Very good.

22
00:02:25,290 --> 00:02:26,290
Thank you.

23
00:02:26,770 --> 00:02:27,455
Sir.

24
00:02:27,470 --> 00:02:29,315
Okay, guys. What do we got on deck?

25
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:31,680
Progress report on two of
the open investigations.

26
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:35,105
Ari Spyros from the S.E.C...

27
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:35,825
Call back.

28
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:36,960
is here.

29
00:02:37,310 --> 00:02:38,705
I'm sorry, sir. He... he wouldn't...

30
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:40,810
It's fine, Kim. It's okay. Thank you.

31
00:02:41,690 --> 00:02:43,335
Well, Spyros.

32
00:02:43,350 --> 00:02:44,520
What's the occasion?

33
00:02:44,630 --> 00:02:45,995
We're not interviewing right now.

34
00:02:46,010 --> 00:02:48,070
Funny. Here.

35
00:02:50,290 --> 00:02:52,280
This got your panties all sticky?

36
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:53,550
Drenched.

37
00:02:53,810 --> 00:02:57,330
Suspect trading pattern
on Pepsum Pharmaceuticals.

38
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:01,880
One of my grunts riding the Midas
spotted a days-long buy spike.

39
00:03:05,570 --> 00:03:06,710
Yeah, get me Spyros.

40
00:03:08,130 --> 00:03:09,130
Take a look.

41
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:16,245
You can all study the charts, or I
can give you the answers to the test.

42
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:18,905
- Please.
- Lenny Bosco... Old Oaks Investments.

43
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:21,275
Peter Decker... Quaker Ridge Financial.

44
00:03:21,290 --> 00:03:24,265
Dan Margolis... Century Capital.

45
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:28,745
These three small firms all knew exactly
when to buy and when to sell the stock.

46
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:30,195
They had inside information.

47
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:31,865
You must get pings like that every day.

48
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:32,775
We do.

49
00:03:32,790 --> 00:03:34,890
So? Get them talking and fine them.

50
00:03:35,110 --> 00:03:36,530
It's bigger than that.

51
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:41,730
All three firms have
links to Bobby Axelrod.

52
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:49,760
Bobby fucking Axelrod.
Man of the people.

53
00:03:51,940 --> 00:03:54,830
You only have that exact look
on your face when you're here.

54
00:03:54,880 --> 00:03:56,790
The pizza's just really fucking good.

55
00:03:57,190 --> 00:03:58,300
Take a bite.

56
00:04:03,150 --> 00:04:04,095
It's good.

57
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:06,620
Good? That is a thing of beauty.

58
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:08,820
That's why we lived in
here when we were kids.

59
00:04:10,250 --> 00:04:12,580
I don't usually open for breakfast,
guys.

60
00:04:12,850 --> 00:04:14,030
You want another?

61
00:04:15,050 --> 00:04:16,050
I do.

62
00:04:17,140 --> 00:04:18,260
We want 'em all.

63
00:04:19,530 --> 00:04:20,530
All?

64
00:04:23,490 --> 00:04:24,865
I want to come in with you.

65
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:25,960
Partner up.

66
00:04:27,290 --> 00:04:28,990
What the fuck did you tell him, kid?

67
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:30,835
I don't want no charity.

68
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:32,995
Look, all I told him was the truth,
Bruno, okay?

69
00:04:33,010 --> 00:04:36,445
That I got a call from a guy who said he
was coming in here with a falafel shop,

70
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:38,375
and would I contract the rebuild.

71
00:04:38,390 --> 00:04:39,795
And when I asked you about it,

72
00:04:39,810 --> 00:04:41,985
you said that you were getting
squeezed by a new landlord.

73
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,535
And when I got that call,
I made a couple calls of my own.

74
00:04:44,550 --> 00:04:46,410
Falafel shop goes in the mall.

75
00:04:46,490 --> 00:04:50,530
We lock in you in on a 20-year lease,
and I cover the overage.

76
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:52,415
I-I-I don't know, Bobby.

77
00:04:52,430 --> 00:04:53,745
Come on. What is there to know?

78
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:55,825
You let me slide for
weeks without paying

79
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:58,265
when I was coming in here
every day after school.

80
00:04:58,280 --> 00:04:59,985
That was just
'cause you were a good customer.

81
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:01,570
Which I want to keep being.

82
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:03,390
Nothing changes.

83
00:05:11,270 --> 00:05:13,410
Thank you. Thank you.

84
00:05:14,030 --> 00:05:16,720
I'd love to tell you I knew you'd
grow up to become what you did,

85
00:05:17,050 --> 00:05:21,475
but to be honest, I had no idea.

86
00:05:21,490 --> 00:05:23,000
That makes two of us.

87
00:05:23,270 --> 00:05:24,270
Come here.

88
00:05:26,410 --> 00:05:27,410
Thanks.

89
00:05:36,290 --> 00:05:38,865
Bobby,
we are ready to roll on something sweet.

90
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:40,035
I think you'll want to piggyback.

91
00:05:40,050 --> 00:05:40,805
What do you got?

92
00:05:40,820 --> 00:05:42,805
Lumetherm Power getting
bought by Electric Sun.

93
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:45,375
Price is $41. Stock's trading at $35.

94
00:05:45,390 --> 00:05:48,415
We're looking at a 17% bump in
two weeks when the deal closes.

95
00:05:48,430 --> 00:05:49,895
Annualized, that's $442...

96
00:05:49,910 --> 00:05:50,785
I'd love to size up.

97
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:53,685
Maybe you buy 2 million
shares for the main fund.

98
00:05:53,700 --> 00:05:55,380
- Sounds about right.
- Great.

99
00:05:55,560 --> 00:05:58,480
Scott Kazawitz's name is being
floated as the new chairman.

100
00:05:58,860 --> 00:06:00,070
Kazawitz.

101
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:02,705
That's a new piece of information.

102
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:04,025
Well, that's what you pay me for.

103
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:05,445
Who said this deal is gonna close?

104
00:06:05,460 --> 00:06:06,875
Ben said it would this morning.

105
00:06:06,890 --> 00:06:08,905
Me? Everyone's saying it.

106
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:09,930
Who is this?

107
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:11,270
My new analyst.

108
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:13,810
Well, if we hired you,
you must be a genius.

109
00:06:14,210 --> 00:06:14,895
Yale?

110
00:06:14,910 --> 00:06:16,445
Stanford. Then Wharton.

111
00:06:16,460 --> 00:06:17,870
Okay, Stanford-Wharton.

112
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:20,680
Electric Sun is controlled by Kazawitz.

113
00:06:20,760 --> 00:06:23,185
He also owns 19.3% of Lumetherm

114
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:25,670
backdoored through his
stake in Southern Wind.

115
00:06:25,930 --> 00:06:28,275
You see that block trade last
Thursday come out of Merrill?

116
00:06:28,290 --> 00:06:31,590
Yeah. That was Fortress cashing
out their shorts before the merger.

117
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:33,295
Wasn't it?

118
00:06:33,310 --> 00:06:35,425
Trade was at 12:52,
when everyone was at lunch,

119
00:06:35,440 --> 00:06:37,680
which tells me they
wanted it to be missed.

120
00:06:37,850 --> 00:06:40,220
You guys caught it,
which is something, I guess.

121
00:06:40,390 --> 00:06:41,780
But you're looking at it backward.

122
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:45,780
Electric Sun's offer was just a ploy
to temporarily prop up Lumetherm.

123
00:06:45,830 --> 00:06:48,655
Typical Kazawitz play to bail on a loser.
He's an animal.

124
00:06:48,670 --> 00:06:51,625
The block trade was Kazawitz
getting out of Southern Wind,

125
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:53,200
getting out of Lumetherm.

126
00:06:53,510 --> 00:06:56,155
He rode the story, now he's out,
which means you need to be out.

127
00:06:56,170 --> 00:06:57,615
In fact, short.

128
00:06:57,630 --> 00:07:00,230
It'll slide to $32 and
change after word breaks.

129
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:03,990
That's a good catch, Axe.

130
00:07:06,830 --> 00:07:08,510
My cholesterol's high enough.

131
00:07:08,930 --> 00:07:11,280
Don't butter my ass, Danzig.
Just get smarter.

132
00:07:12,790 --> 00:07:15,050
Your read was good with
the information you had.

133
00:07:15,150 --> 00:07:17,390
You're new. You'll figure it out.

134
00:07:17,930 --> 00:07:19,050
Or you'll be gone.

135
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:22,525
Jesus Christ.

136
00:07:22,540 --> 00:07:25,390
Yeah. And he went to Hofstra.

137
00:07:26,580 --> 00:07:29,440
This is big. It's criminal.

138
00:07:29,650 --> 00:07:32,475
And I want to be a part
of treeing Bobby Axelrod.

139
00:07:32,490 --> 00:07:34,045
Hey, I want to be an astronaut,

140
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:36,980
but, uh, you know,
I get airsick in choppy weather.

141
00:07:37,410 --> 00:07:39,065
Your burden of proof is lower.

142
00:07:39,080 --> 00:07:42,755
They'll talk to you because they're
happy to pay a fine and move on.

143
00:07:42,770 --> 00:07:44,220
I'm at the coalface.

144
00:07:44,790 --> 00:07:47,665
And I really don't appreciate
you strolling in here

145
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:49,585
and telling me how to
deploy my resources

146
00:07:49,600 --> 00:07:52,105
because you can't build
your own fucking case.

147
00:07:52,120 --> 00:07:53,505
I get it. You guys are rock stars.

148
00:07:53,520 --> 00:07:55,295
Yeah, we get it.
You want to be one, too.

149
00:07:55,310 --> 00:07:56,185
None of that matters.

150
00:07:56,200 --> 00:08:00,115
Uh, Spyros, if you've locked on
to some trail of bread crumbs

151
00:08:00,130 --> 00:08:03,535
that leads to Bobby Axelrod,
that could be a win for everyone.

152
00:08:03,550 --> 00:08:06,435
So, leave your stuff, we'll take a look,

153
00:08:06,450 --> 00:08:09,615
and we'll let you know if it rises
to the level that we can prosecute,

154
00:08:09,630 --> 00:08:12,065
one that won't be reversed on appeal.

155
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:16,065
You know, I understand the
source of your reluctance.

156
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:17,520
I have a wife, too.

157
00:08:19,010 --> 00:08:19,705
But it's your job...

158
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:21,310
Hey, you got your answer.

159
00:08:21,740 --> 00:08:22,740
We're done.

160
00:08:51,300 --> 00:08:55,235
I loved and remember each and
every one of your fathers,

161
00:08:55,250 --> 00:08:59,500
so I'm proud of all of you who are
ready to head off to school in the fall.

162
00:08:59,970 --> 00:09:02,710
26 of ours put through college

163
00:09:02,900 --> 00:09:05,210
by the Memorial Foundation so far.

164
00:09:05,250 --> 00:09:07,915
And this year's group...
so let's bring them up,

165
00:09:07,930 --> 00:09:09,845
and let's give them a round of
applause for their achievement.

166
00:09:09,860 --> 00:09:12,040
Freddie Aquafino, off to Duke.

167
00:09:17,050 --> 00:09:18,180
Freddie, Freddie.

168
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:20,920
I traded shoulder to
shoulder with your father.

169
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:23,710
He'd be so proud of you today.

170
00:09:26,280 --> 00:09:28,070
First year's tuition and board.

171
00:09:28,570 --> 00:09:29,710
All right, come here.

172
00:09:33,750 --> 00:09:35,600
That's what this is all about, guys.

173
00:09:35,830 --> 00:09:38,600
Not only how our firm survived 9/11,

174
00:09:39,010 --> 00:09:40,440
but how we didn't give up.

175
00:09:40,850 --> 00:09:43,920
How we committed to each other,
to our family.

176
00:09:45,460 --> 00:09:48,175
And I am so moved by how
we've all flourished together.

177
00:09:48,190 --> 00:09:50,150
Some flourishing more than others.

178
00:09:52,330 --> 00:09:56,065
It's just wrong that you're
the one standing there.

179
00:09:56,080 --> 00:09:57,355
All right, that's enough.

180
00:09:57,370 --> 00:09:59,060
No, no. It's okay. June...

181
00:10:01,670 --> 00:10:03,040
I'd feel the same way.

182
00:10:03,750 --> 00:10:04,750
I get it.

183
00:10:05,260 --> 00:10:07,980
You got to be thinking,
"Why was he spared?

184
00:10:08,310 --> 00:10:09,690
Why is my husband gone?"

185
00:10:10,420 --> 00:10:14,000
For months after the planes hit,
all I could do was ask myself why.

186
00:10:15,810 --> 00:10:17,395
Why was I the only surviving partner?

187
00:10:17,410 --> 00:10:18,475
Why wasn't I there?

188
00:10:18,490 --> 00:10:20,275
Could... could I have done something?

189
00:10:20,290 --> 00:10:23,520
Why was I the only one out of the
office on meetings that morning?

190
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:26,930
We'll never know.

191
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:30,710
So I made up my own "Why."

192
00:10:32,690 --> 00:10:33,960
Because of you.

193
00:10:35,960 --> 00:10:37,960
You put it all on your back, Bobby.

194
00:10:40,330 --> 00:10:42,040
Am I the greatest guy in the world?

195
00:10:42,460 --> 00:10:43,650
Hell, no.

196
00:10:44,060 --> 00:10:46,595
I just did what I knew the
partners and friends I'd lost

197
00:10:46,610 --> 00:10:48,230
would've done for my family.

198
00:10:49,760 --> 00:10:51,620
What your husband would've done, June.

199
00:10:54,410 --> 00:10:55,880
And you know I'm sorry.

200
00:11:02,470 --> 00:11:04,710
'Cause I miss Rake every day...

201
00:11:07,960 --> 00:11:10,240
just as my beautiful wife, Lara,

202
00:11:11,170 --> 00:11:13,420
misses her heroic brother Dean.

203
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:21,620
Bobby Axelrod is Mike
Tyson in his prime.

204
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:24,830
And you do not want
Mike Tyson in his prime.

205
00:11:25,070 --> 00:11:26,875
Remember what happened to
the guys who fought him then?

206
00:11:26,890 --> 00:11:28,295
Yeah, they got their faces pushed in.

207
00:11:28,310 --> 00:11:30,040
But eventually he got beat.

208
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:32,620
Buster Douglas knocked him on his ass.
- Right. - Ketchup?

209
00:11:32,930 --> 00:11:35,310
"Eventually" is the key word.

210
00:11:35,350 --> 00:11:37,180
When Tyson was ready to go.

211
00:11:38,140 --> 00:11:41,325
Since my appointment, this office is
undefeated in financial prosecutions...

212
00:11:41,340 --> 00:11:42,455
81 and 0.

213
00:11:42,470 --> 00:11:44,775
And that's because I know
when the time is right.

214
00:11:44,790 --> 00:11:48,420
I get it, but...
this would be a big one.

215
00:11:49,100 --> 00:11:50,980
Bryan, what do you think
happened here this morning?

216
00:11:52,100 --> 00:11:53,900
We got significant information.

217
00:11:54,210 --> 00:11:57,090
Maybe. Or maybe it was
Spyros setting us up.

218
00:11:58,340 --> 00:12:01,485
Now, we go in first and
lose in the criminal action,

219
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:04,170
Spyros in the S.E.C. can
still get him civilly.

220
00:12:04,710 --> 00:12:07,830
Now, we win, Spyros wins. We lose...

221
00:12:08,320 --> 00:12:09,965
Spyros still fucking wins.

222
00:12:09,980 --> 00:12:12,005
Damn. The ketchup's empty.

223
00:12:12,020 --> 00:12:14,850
We've got to be playing
three-dimensional chess.

224
00:12:15,820 --> 00:12:18,695
Axe is a folk hero in this town.

225
00:12:18,710 --> 00:12:21,305
The guy gave the New York
City Firefighter's Foundation

226
00:12:21,320 --> 00:12:23,825
$100 million last year.

227
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:27,265
Police gave him a plaque at Ground
Zero with his goddamn name on it.

228
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:29,315
Spitzer's name was on all
the highway signs, too.

229
00:12:29,330 --> 00:12:30,700
Signs come down.

230
00:12:31,750 --> 00:12:33,310
That's why I love you, man.

231
00:12:34,160 --> 00:12:38,140
But a good matador doesn't
try to kill a fresh bull.

232
00:12:39,610 --> 00:12:42,390
You wait until he's
been stuck a few times.

233
00:12:44,420 --> 00:12:46,710
We need an opening, however small.

234
00:12:46,800 --> 00:12:50,685
Then we get the guy the moment that
he's gettable, just like the others.

235
00:12:50,700 --> 00:12:52,820
But not if there's a chance we lose.

236
00:12:56,300 --> 00:12:58,630
What about... the other issue?

237
00:12:59,730 --> 00:13:01,960
The one Spyros mentioned... home?

238
00:13:04,810 --> 00:13:06,070
Was he right about that?

239
00:13:07,250 --> 00:13:08,250
No.

240
00:13:17,530 --> 00:13:18,985
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

241
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,285
I-I don't...
- I don't know what... - You know what, it's a tough day

242
00:13:21,300 --> 00:13:22,360
for everyone.

243
00:13:23,180 --> 00:13:24,220
Just...

244
00:13:25,570 --> 00:13:27,720
I just had to sell the Oceanis.

245
00:13:30,040 --> 00:13:31,860
Rake just loved that boat.

246
00:13:32,420 --> 00:13:34,540
I am sure that's why it upset you.

247
00:13:35,230 --> 00:13:38,230
You know, me, I never set foot on a
yacht until I started dating Bobby.

248
00:13:38,880 --> 00:13:43,120
In Inwood, growing up, the only boating
we ever did was the Staten Island Ferry.

249
00:13:44,620 --> 00:13:46,650
Big Irish family. Five sibs.

250
00:13:46,960 --> 00:13:48,170
Close, though.

251
00:13:48,720 --> 00:13:51,010
Firemen, cops, nurses.

252
00:13:52,700 --> 00:13:54,340
Then, when I moved up here,

253
00:13:54,840 --> 00:13:58,100
this world,
I saw how everyone looked at me.

254
00:13:58,680 --> 00:13:59,890
I never judged.

255
